        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <a href="/guides" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-gopro-orange transition-colors">Guides</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <span class="text-white">Drywall Repair After Storms</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-4 tracking-tight">Drywall Repair After Florida Storms</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-3xl font-sans">
        Fixing water-damaged drywall after seasonal storms. When to patch, when to replace, and mold prevention.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-padding">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
          <p class="text-xl text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6 font-sans">
            Florida's hurricane season and afternoon thunderstorms bring heavy rain, wind-driven water, and occasional roof damage. After a storm, <a href="/services/drywall" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">drywall</a> water damage shows up as ceiling stains, sagging drywall, peeling paint, or soggy walls. Here's how to assess and repair storm-damaged drywall in <a href="/areas/central-florida" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Central Florida</a>.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Common Storm Damage to Drywall</h2>
          
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">1. Roof Leaks (Ceiling Damage)</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Missing shingles, lifted flashing, or clogged gutters let water into attics. Ceiling drywall absorbs water, gets heavy, and eventually sags or collapses. You'll see brown water stains, bulging ceiling texture, or dripping.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">2. Wind-Driven Rain (Wall Damage)</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Tropical storms and hurricanes drive rain horizontally into windows, doors, and vents. Water infiltrates wall cavities, soaking drywall from behind. Walls feel damp, paint bubbles, or drywall turns soft.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">3. Plumbing Failures During Storms</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Power outages and pressure surges during storms can cause burst pipes or water heater leaks. Water soaks ceilings and walls from inside the home. See our <a href="/guides/water-damage-drywall-repair-central-florida" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">water damage repair guide</a>.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Assessing Drywall Damage After a Storm</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Before repairing, you need to know how bad the damage is. Here's what to check:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Visual Inspection</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Stains:</strong> Brown or yellow water rings on ceilings or walls</li>
            <li><strong>Sagging:</strong> Ceiling drywall droops or bows downward</li>
            <li><strong>Bubbling paint or texture:</strong> Surface peels or blisters</li>
            <li><strong>Soft spots:</strong> Drywall feels spongy when pressed</li>
            <li><strong>Mold or musty smell:</strong> Black or green spots, damp odor</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Touch Test</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Press your hand against the damaged area. If it feels damp, cool to the touch, or your finger sinks in slightly, the drywall is still wet or has absorbed enough water to lose structural integrity. Wet or soft drywall must be replaced—it won't dry back to original strength.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Moisture Meter (Optional but Helpful)</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            A cheap pin-type moisture meter ($20-$40 at hardware stores) tells you if drywall is still wet behind paint. Anything above 15-17% moisture needs time to dry or replacement.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gopro-orange/10 border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
              <strong>Safety first:</strong> If ceiling drywall is sagging or bulging, don't stand directly under it—it can fall. If you see active leaks or heavy sagging, call a contractor immediately. Don't delay; ceiling collapses cause injury and more damage.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">When to Patch vs. Replace Drywall</h2>
          
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Patch & Repaint (Minor Damage)</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            If the leak is small, caught quickly, and drywall is dry and solid:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li>Small water stains (no sagging or soft spots)</li>
            <li>Drywall feels firm, not spongy</li>
            <li>No mold smell or visible mold</li>
            <li>Dried completely within 48-72 hours</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            We seal the stain with stain-blocking primer (Kilz or BIN), patch any surface cracks, match texture, and <a href="/services/interior-exterior-painting" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">repaint</a>. Cost: $150-$400 per small area.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Replace Drywall (Moderate to Severe Damage)</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Replace drywall if:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Sagging or bowing:</strong> Ceiling or wall visibly droops</li>
            <li><strong>Soft or spongy:</strong> Drywall feels weak when pressed</li>
            <li><strong>Stayed wet more than 48 hours:</strong> Even if it eventually dried, prolonged moisture compromises strength</li>
            <li><strong>Mold visible or suspected:</strong> Black, green, or fuzzy growth behind paint or drywall</li>
            <li><strong>Insulation is soaked:</strong> Wet insulation above ceiling drywall means replacement</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            We cut out damaged sections, remove wet insulation, dry the cavity (fans, dehumidifiers), install new drywall, tape seams, texture-match, prime, and paint. Cost depends on area; see our <a href="/guides/drywall-repair-cost-kissimmee" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">drywall repair cost guide</a>.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Mold Concerns in Florida Humidity</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Florida's year-round humidity accelerates mold growth. After storm water intrusion, mold can appear in 24-48 hours if drywall stays wet. Here's how to prevent it:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Dry Quickly</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Fans and dehumidifiers:</strong> Run continuously until walls and ceilings are dry (48-72 hours)</li>
            <li><strong>AC on:</strong> Air conditioning pulls moisture out of the air—keep it running even if house is cool</li>
            <li><strong>Remove wet materials:</strong> Pull up soaked carpet, remove wet baseboards, cut out soggy drywall</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Check Behind Walls and Ceilings</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Just because the surface dried doesn't mean the wall cavity is dry. If drywall was soaked, we cut inspection holes to check insulation and studs. Wet insulation needs removal—it won't dry properly and grows mold.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Use Mold-Resistant Materials</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            When replacing storm-damaged drywall, we recommend mold-resistant drywall (purple or green board) for moisture-prone areas—bathrooms, ceilings under flat roofs, exterior walls. Costs slightly more but resists mold better than standard drywall.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Typical Repair Timeline</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Timing matters after a storm. Here's a realistic schedule:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-none space-y-2 mb-6 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Day 0-1:</strong> Stop the leak (roof repair, seal windows). Remove standing water, start drying.</li>
            <li><strong>Day 2-3:</strong> Continue drying with fans and dehumidifiers. Assess damage—minor stain or full replacement?</li>
            <li><strong>Day 4-7:</strong> Once dry, cut out damaged drywall, replace sections, tape seams. Allow mud/compound to dry (Florida humidity slows this—add 1-2 days).</li>
            <li><strong>Day 8-10:</strong> Sand, texture-match, prime, paint. Allow paint to cure.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Total timeline: 7-14 days from storm to finished repair, depending on damage extent and drying time. Larger jobs (multiple rooms, ceilings) take 2-3 weeks.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Insurance Claims for Storm Damage</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Homeowner's insurance covers storm damage if the cause is covered (wind, hail, falling tree). Flood damage requires separate flood insurance. Here's how to handle claims:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Document everything:</strong> Photos of damage, roof leaks, wet drywall before removal</li>
            <li><strong>Get estimates:</strong> We provide itemized estimates for insurance adjusters</li>
            <li><strong>Don't delay repairs:</strong> Insurers expect you to mitigate damage quickly (dry things out, prevent further damage)</li>
            <li><strong>Save damaged materials:</strong> Keep samples of wet drywall and insulation for adjuster inspection</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Preventing Future Storm Damage</h2>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Roof maintenance:</strong> Inspect and repair shingles, flashing, and skylights annually</li>
            <li><strong>Seal windows and doors:</strong> Replace worn weatherstripping and caulk</li>
            <li><strong>Clean gutters:</strong> Clogged gutters overflow and soak walls during heavy rain</li>
            <li><strong>Trim trees:</strong> Keep branches away from roof to prevent wind damage</li>
            <li><strong>Check attic ventilation:</strong> Proper airflow helps dry out moisture after leaks</li>
          </ul>

          <div class="text-center mt-12">
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 mb-6 font-sans">Need storm-damaged drywall repaired in Central Florida?</p>
            <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
